\name{cellucid_figure_export_set}
\alias{cellucid_figure_export_set}
\title{Change figure export settings}
\usage{
cellucid_figure_export_set(
  viewer,
  auto_text_size,
  background,
  background_color,
  depth_sort,
  download,
  dpi,
  emphasize_selection,
  export_all_views,
  font_family,
  font_sizes,
  frame,
  include_axes,
  include_legend,
  legend_position,
  optimized_target_count,
  preview,
  reference_grid,
  selection_muted_opacity,
  show_orientation,
  size_preset,
  svg_strategy,
  title,
  title_follows_dataset,
  width,
  x_label,
  y_label
)
}
\arguments{
\item{viewer}{A viewer \code{\link{cellucid_view}()} or \code{\link{cellucid_serve}()} returned.}
\item{auto_text_size}{Set the text in the sizes journals print figure text at: 7 pt, ticks and centroid labels 6 pt, the title 8 pt (font_sizes is then null). \code{TRUE} or \code{FALSE}. Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
\item{background}{The background: the viewer's, white, transparent or custom. One of \code{"viewer"}, \code{"white"}, \code{"transparent"}, \code{"custom"}. Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
\item{background_color}{The custom background color, lowercase #rrggbb. Text. Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
\item{depth_sort}{Draw 3D points back to front. \code{TRUE} or \code{FALSE}. Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
\item{download}{What Export downloads. One of \code{"svg"}, \code{"png"}, \code{"svg+png"}, \code{"png-multi"}, \code{"all"}. Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
\item{dpi}{The resolution of a PNG: its pixels are the width in inches times this (an SVG is the same size, as vectors). One of \code{150}, \code{300}, \code{600}. Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
\item{emphasize_selection}{Fade the cells that are not highlighted. \code{TRUE} or \code{FALSE}. Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
\item{export_all_views}{Export every view of the multiview, side by side: each panel is titled by its view's name (view_rename; a view with none by its field and filters). \code{TRUE} or \code{FALSE}. Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
\item{font_family}{The font of every text. One of \code{"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"}, \code{"Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif"}, \code{"\\"Times New Roman\\", Times, serif"}. Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
\item{font_sizes}{Text sizes in points (1/72 inch; halves allowed) while auto_text_size is off, else null. They keep their printed size at any width and resolution. A named list or \code{NULL}. Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does). \describe{\item{\code{axis_label}}{Axis labels. A number (at least 1, at most 375). Required.}\item{\code{base}}{Panel titles of a multi-panel figure ("A. Cell type") and the other small labels (orientation, selection badge). A number (at least 1, at most 375). Required.}\item{\code{centroid_label}}{Category labels drawn at their cells' centers. A number (at least 1, at most 375). Required.}\item{\code{legend}}{The legend's title and entries. A number (at least 1, at most 375). Required.}\item{\code{ticks}}{Axis tick labels. A number (at least 1, at most 375). Required.}\item{\code{title}}{The figure's title above everything (not the panel titles: base sizes those). A number (at least 1, at most 375). Required.}}}
\item{frame}{The part of the view exported (needs the preview on, which "cells" turns on), null for the whole view, or "cells": the frame every cell the figure draws fills, with a small margin (the panel's Fit the cells), so a plot takes the cells' shape instead of its pane's (a narrow pane gives a narrow plot; never rearrange the person's screen for a figure). With export_all_views, one frame holding every panel's cells. Either a named list or \code{NULL} (The part of the view exported (needs the preview on), or null for the whole view); or exactly \code{"cells"}. Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does). \describe{\item{\code{lock_aspect}}{Keep the frame's shape when it is resized. \code{TRUE} or \code{FALSE}. Required.}\item{\code{rect}}{The frame as fractions of the view, from its top left. A named list. Required. \describe{\item{\code{height}}{A number (at least 0, at most 1). Required.}\item{\code{width}}{A number (at least 0, at most 1). Required.}\item{\code{x}}{A number (at least 0, at most 1). Required.}\item{\code{y}}{A number (at least 0, at most 1). Required.}}}}}
\item{include_axes}{Draw the axes. \code{TRUE} or \code{FALSE}. Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
\item{include_legend}{Draw the legend. \code{TRUE} or \code{FALSE}. Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
\item{legend_position}{Where the legend goes. One of \code{"right"}, \code{"bottom"}. Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
\item{optimized_target_count}{How many points an optimized SVG keeps. A whole number (at least 1000, at most 5000000). Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
\item{preview}{The preview in the panel and the color-vision deficiency it simulates; with the preview on, view.picture with source "export" draws that simulation too (Export writes the true colors). A named list. Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does). \describe{\item{\code{colorblind_mode}}{One of \code{"none"}, \code{"deuteranopia"}, \code{"protanopia"}, \code{"tritanopia"}. Required.}\item{\code{enabled}}{\code{TRUE} or \code{FALSE}. Required.}}}
\item{reference_grid}{Draw a reference grid. \code{TRUE} or \code{FALSE}. Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
\item{selection_muted_opacity}{The opacity of the faded cells, 0 to 1. A number (at least 0, at most 1). Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
\item{show_orientation}{Draw the 3D orientation indicator. \code{TRUE} or \code{FALSE}. Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
\item{size_preset}{The figure's width as a journal column: single-column (89 mm), one-and-half-column (120 mm), double-column (183 mm); custom is the width given. A preset sets width. One of \code{"single-column"}, \code{"one-and-half-column"}, \code{"double-column"}, \code{"custom"}. Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
\item{svg_strategy}{How an SVG draws its points: full-vector (the default: every point a circle, editable; a file grows with the cells drawn), optimized-vector (fewer points, optimized_target_count), hybrid (the points as one image, text and axes as vector: small at any cell count). One of \code{"full-vector"}, \code{"optimized-vector"}, \code{"hybrid"}. Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
\item{title}{The figure title while it does not follow the dataset, else null. Text or \code{NULL}. Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
\item{title_follows_dataset}{The title is the dataset's name (title is then null). \code{TRUE} or \code{FALSE}. Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
\item{width}{The figure's width in millimetres; the figure is exactly this wide, at every resolution, and as tall as its plots make it (each keeps its view's shape, or its frame's: set a frame to change the shape). A width that is not a preset's makes size_preset custom. A number (at least 10). Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
\item{x_label}{The x axis label. Text. Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
\item{y_label}{The y axis label. Text. Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
}
\value{
A \code{cellucid_action_request}, at once and invisibly: the action is sent now, or when the viewer's channel opens.
\code{\link{cellucid_action_result}(request)} returns its outcome (what it did, as names, counts and ids), or \code{NULL} when it had nothing to report; \code{request$step} is the history step it made.
}
\description{
Set Figure export options: width (millimetres or a journal column; the figure fills it, text in points keeps its printed size), frame (frame "cells" fits it to the cells, so plots take their shape, not the pane's), background, fonts, title and axis labels, axes, legend, preview, what Export downloads; only those given change, nothing is exported (figure-export.get reads them; view.picture with source "export" shows the figure they make).
}
\details{
The viewer's action \code{figure-export.set} (Settings). It changes what the viewer shows: one step of the viewer's history, labeled as R's, undone like any other. The state it can change: \code{settings}.
}
\seealso{
\code{\link{cellucid_act}()} sends any action by its type, and \code{\link{cellucid_action_result}()}
collects its answer.
}
\examples{
if (interactive() && requireNamespace("SeuratObject", quietly = TRUE)) {
  data("pbmc_small", package = "SeuratObject")
  viewer <- cellucid_view(SeuratObject::UpdateSeuratObject(pbmc_small))
  request <- cellucid_figure_export_set(viewer, include_axes = FALSE,
      size_preset = "double-column")
  print(request)
  # cellucid_action_result(request) waits for its outcome.
  cellucid_stop(viewer)
}
}
